58:A common time series foundation model (TSFM) recipe pairs a shared, frozen backbone with a task-specific head. Because downstream series mix heterogeneous regimes that benefit from different aggregation topologies, the natural upgrade is a mixture-of-experts (MoE) head: a router over architecturally distinct experts, one selected per input window. We identify a failure mode of this upgrade that arises with instance normalization. Once any backbone layer is unfrozen, the router converges to a one-hot assignment, routing entropy collapses to $0.000$, and only one expert ever receives a gradient update. We characterize and name this failure mode \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse}. The mechanism is direct: instance-normalization layers (e.g., RevIN) strip the per-window mean and variance the router needs. A $720$-run sweep across SOTA MoE rescue mechanisms (load balancing, z-loss, entropy regularization, ReLU routing, expert-choice) converges to the same collapsed state, recovering at most $10.9\%$ MSE. This remains $2.7\times$ worse than our causal intervention, because the failure is in the router's input, not its optimization. We formalize the mechanism via a mutual-information decomposition. The resulting signal-ratio statistic, computable before training, predicts dataset vulnerability to collapse (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$) and correctly flags boundary cases. The diagnosis prescribes a one-line \emph{causal intervention}: \emph{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters} (RR-MoA) routes on the raw, pre-normalization input. RR-MoA wins $54/54$ dataset/freeze-level cells on the primary backbone ($26$--$79\%$ MSE improvement over the best fixed adapter) and generalizes across four further backbones and an imputation task. Eight causal controls including a vision-modality replication isolate the cause to instance normalization, and the diagnosis predicts a verified \emph{Frozen Paradox}: frozen adapters beat full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$. Applying the same raw-signal principle at the expert level (Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}) closes the remaining gap to per-dataset supervised baselines.
383:\textbf{Expert pool.} We maintain $K{=}5$ topologically distinct expert adapters (mean-pool, last-token, max-pool, attention-pool, Conv1d-pool), spanning the canonical pooling families used in sequence representation learning~\citep{reimers2019sentencebert}. Architectural diversity is critical: replacing the five distinct heads with five identical experts (same topology, different initialization) degrades MSE by $8$--$15\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:diversity}). Pseudocode in Algorithm~\ref{alg:rrmoa} (Appendix~\ref{app:setup}). We now empirically validate that this minimal fix recovers routing diversity, beats the comprehensive baseline suite, and that its predictions hold under eight causal controls.
431:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{The Frozen Paradox.} Frozen RR-MoA beats \emph{full fine-tuning} (all 8 blocks unfrozen, best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 LRs) by $12$--$79\%$ on every dataset (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, Figure~\ref{fig:frozen_paradox}). Two distinct mechanisms contribute. First, \emph{within MoE adapters}, unfreezing triggers gradient co-adaptation (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}): the dominant expert reshapes the shared backbone, collapsing routing, and freezing prevents this loop and preserves expert diversity. Second, \emph{across architectures}, the frozen five-expert mixture with learned per-sample routing provides an architectural advantage that a single unfrozen head cannot match even with full parameter access: per-sample expert selection captures input-regime structure that no single topology can represent. Within RR-MoA, frozen is best on 4/6 datasets; light unfreezing wins by up to $13\%$ on the other two. A second architecture introduced below, \textbf{SR-MoA} (Self-Routed MoA), in which each expert has its own sigmoid gate on raw input rather than a shared external router, independently replicates this pattern (frozen best on 4/6 datasets; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Even with careful hyperparameter tuning (learning rates $10^{-5}$ to $10^{-6}$, 100 epochs, cosine schedule with 10-epoch warmup, layerwise LR decay, weight decay sweep, gradient accumulation up to effective batch 512, 90 configurations), full fine-tuning still loses by $51$--$71\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}).}
495:\textbf{The principle is architecture-agnostic (SR-MoA).} To confirm the bottleneck is in the routing \emph{input} rather than the router \emph{architecture}, SR-MoA~\citep{lv2025aoe,cheng2025ermoe,liu2026routingfree} replaces the external Conv1d router with a per-expert sigmoid gate $\sigma_k(\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw})$, with no external router at all. SR-MoA \emph{outperforms} RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ across all 6 datasets (ETTh1: $0.464$; ETTm1: $0.388$; Weather: $0.222$; 252 runs total), generalizes across six backbones, and independently replicates the Frozen Paradox (frozen best on 4/6 datasets; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). That a different architecture exceeds RR-MoA while sharing only the raw-input principle confirms it is the \emph{principle}, not the specific router design, that drives the gains. The same SR-MoA architecture gating on hidden states degrades by $+87\%$ to $+768\%$ on \textbf{all 6 datasets} ($p{=}0.016$, Wilcoxon). \textbf{Normalization-specificity:} on Timer-XL (no RevIN), the same raw-vs-hidden comparison yields only $+4$--$9\%$ ($15$--$100{\times}$ smaller than on MOMENT), confirming the degradation is normalization-induced. A third architecture-level control, \textbf{AdaMix-Raw} (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}), replaces AdaMix's hidden-state router with a raw-input router and recovers entropy to $1.41$--$1.57$ and MSE by $41$--$88\%$ on all 12 cells. All three router instantiations (RR-MoA, SR-MoA, AdaMix-Raw) are consistent with the same diagnosis: the bottleneck is normalization, not router design. Table~\ref{tab:causal_controls} summarizes all eight controls.
551:RR-MoA outperforms every baseline ($p{<}0.001$). The independent ensemble (same experts, no router) is $36$--$46\%$ worse, confirming learned routing adds value beyond diversity.
553:\textbf{Closing the DLinear gap.} DLinear (49K params, from scratch) outperforms frozen adapters in absolute MSE (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, grey rows). The gap reflects information destruction by the normalization-encoding pipeline (k-NN and Ridge diagnostics in Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostic}), not adapter capacity. As the diagnosis predicts, restoring raw-signal access \emph{at the expert level} closes this gap: \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (a dual-stream architecture in which a shared NLinear raw-input branch is added to each expert's output; Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}, Figure~\ref{fig:residual_ia_arch}) matches or beats DLinear on \textbf{6/6 datasets} at $H{=}96$ (up to $-7.5\%$), generalizing to $107/123$ cells across six backbones. A self-routed variant SR-RIA\textsuperscript{+} (Appendix~\ref{app:sr_ria}) achieves the same 6/6 match-or-beat with $3$ outright wins; a TSFM-free Pure Raw-MLP MoE control (Appendix~\ref{app:raw_mlp_moe}) localizes the TSFM's contribution and rules out the alternative hypothesis that the raw branch alone is doing all the work.
649:\caption{\textbf{Left: Frozen Paradox}, frozen RR-MoA beats full FT by $12$--$79\%$. \textbf{Right: Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} validated} ($\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$). Traffic and Solar are boundary cases.}
1222:\caption{Full LoRA sweep (strictly frozen backbone, 3 seeds, test MSE mean$\pm$std). Bold rows are the best-per-dataset configuration used in Table~\ref{tab:baselines}. No LoRA variant beats the RR-MoA (Top-2) result on any dataset.}
